{
  "mission": "Give you programmatic mastery over agents so you can deploy effective compute anywhere across your stack, products, and engineering lifecycle. By the end you can look at any repeat, domain-specific, or out-of-loop problem and know whether to reach for the out-of-the-box agent, an extended agent, or a complete custom agent — and if custom, build it from a 150-line script up to a multi-agent web application by controlling the core four in code. The deeper mission of the Agentic Horizon: agentic coding is no longer about what YOU can do, it's about what you can teach your agents to do.",
  "progression_model": {
    "the_path": "Agentic engineering leads every engineer down one single path: better agents -> more agents -> custom agents. First you learn to prompt- and context-engineer a single agent well (better). Then you scale compute by delegating to subagents and new primary agents (more). Then, only when you hit domain-specific walls out-of-the-box tools can't clear, you go to the bare metal: custom agents.",
    "better_agents": "Prompt engineering and context engineering a single agent until it performs. This is the foundation the whole Agentic Horizon builds on (RD framework, agentic prompt formats).",
    "more_agents": "Scale your compute to scale your impact by delegating work to subagents and additional primary agents (multi-agent orchestration).",
    "custom_agents": "The bare metal. Build agents whose context, model, prompt, and tools you control in code — tailored to your domain-specific problems, protected from calling the wrong tools at the wrong time, and deployable programmatically anywhere (scripts, streams, terminals, UIs, backends)."
  },
  "core_four_control": {
    "context": "Everything the agent sees costs tokens: system prompt, user prompt, every tool definition, every MCP config (the Firecrawl MCP is ~6k tokens). disallowed_tools removes built-in tools from the context entirely (Calc strips all 15). Adopt the agent's perspective; run /context; only load what the domain needs. clear_context (Ultra Stream) lets an agent reset its own window to run forever.",
    "model": "Declared explicitly per agent. Haiku (claude-3-5-haiku-20241022) for the trivial Echo transform (cheap/fast); Sonnet (claude-sonnet-4-20250514) as the default balanced choice for most agents; Opus (claude-opus-4-1-20250805) offered for the SDLC agent's heavier planning/review. Right-size intelligence to the task and to cost-at-scale.",
    "prompt": "Two prompts now matter: the SYSTEM prompt (the law — affects every run; overwrite for a custom agent, append to extend) and the USER prompt (arrives via client.query()). System-prompt variables are static (locked at boot, e.g. {NOTIFICATION_CRITERIA}, {NUMBER_OF_VERSIONS}); user-prompt variables are dynamic ($1/$ARGUMENTS). Domain agents may embed the whole workflow in the system prompt.",
    "tools": "allowed_tools whitelists (including MCP tools by mcp__<server>__<tool>); disallowed_tools blacklists and de-contexts built-ins; @tool + create_sdk_mcp_server add in-memory custom tools; .mcp.json configs load external MCP servers. Inside a tool you are in deterministic code — return the proper content format, and surface errors so the agent can pivot (stdout + tools leverage point).",
    "why_it_matters": "Custom agents are just the ability to set the core four in code and have the agent perform that way every time, consistently and reproducibly. Isolate the core four in any agent's options and you understand how that agent will behave — and how to scale it. Dan: it's no longer the models that bottleneck us; it's our ability to wield better, more, and custom agents."
  },
  "sdk_mechanics": {
    "package": "claude-agent-sdk (Python; imported as claude_agent_sdk). Formerly the 'Claude Code SDK' / claude_code_sdk — Dan says 'Claude Code SDK' throughout the recording, and the on-screen code still reads ClaudeCodeOptions, but every pyproject.toml and .py file in the shipped repo now imports claude_agent_sdk and ClaudeAgentOptions. Prompt caching is built in, so you save money out of the box.",
    "universal_flow": "1) Build ClaudeAgentOptions (the core four). 2) Mount the agent (query() OR ClaudeSDKClient). 3) Send the user prompt (client.query(...)). 4) Manage the streamed responses (parse blocks; capture session_id/cost from ResultMessage).",
    "query_vs_client": "query(prompt, options) -> async iterator, one-off, NO custom tools. ClaudeSDKClient(options) -> async context manager, multi-turn, REQUIRED for custom tools; call client.query() one or more times and iterate client.receive_response().",
    "message_and_block_types": "AssistantMessage.content = [TextBlock (answer), ThinkingBlock (reasoning, e.g. 'think hard'), ToolUseBlock (name/input), ToolResultBlock (content/is_error)]; ResultMessage (session_id, total_cost_usd, duration_ms, duration_api_ms, num_turns, is_error); SystemMessage (init info incl. tool list). Dan: these may change; learn to parse the primitives.",
    "session_continuity": "Capture ResultMessage.session_id, pass it back as ClaudeAgentOptions(resume=session_id) to continue a conversation across queries (Calc REPL, QA, Tri-Copy, SDLC).",
    "hooks": "hooks={'PreToolUse': [HookMatcher(matcher='Read', hooks=[fn]), HookMatcher(hooks=[fn])], 'PostToolUse': [...]}. A hook fn returns {} to allow, or {'hookSpecificOutput': {'hookEventName': 'PreToolUse', 'permissionDecision': 'deny', 'permissionDecisionReason': '...'}} to block. Works for subagents.",
    "custom_tools": "@tool(name, description, {param: type}) async fn(args: dict) -> {'content': [{'type':'text','text':...}], 'is_error'?: bool}; register via create_sdk_mcp_server(name, version, tools=[...]) and expose with allowed_tools=['mcp__<server>__<tool>'].",
    "system_prompt_forms": "String -> full overwrite (custom agent). Dict {'type':'preset','preset':'claude_code'} -> pure Claude Code (SDLC builder). Dict {'type':'preset','preset':'claude_code','append': '...'} -> extend Claude Code (SDLC planner/reviewer). External .mcp.json path loads MCP servers.",
    "other_options_seen": "model, allowed_tools, disallowed_tools, mcp_servers, max_turns (Social Hype=3), cwd (SDLC agents run in the ticket's codebase), permission_mode ('acceptEdits' for planner/reviewer, 'bypassPermissions' for builder), resume."
  },
  "step_by_step_demo": [
    "(0:07-3:20) Thesis: the single path is better -> more -> custom agents. The mismatch problem: out-of-the-box agents are built for everyone's codebase, not yours, costing hundreds of hours and millions of tokens. Custom agents let you pass your domain knowledge to your compute and take one-agent/one-prompt/one-purpose to its limit.",
    "(3:24-6:45) Pong agent: run pong_agent.py; whatever you type ('hello', 'summarize this codebase', 'king') it replies 'pong'. Open PONG_AGENT_SYSTEM_PROMPT.md — a 3-line overwrite. Two SDK changes (model + system prompt) build a new product. 'This is not Claude Code anymore.' Parse AssistantMessage/TextBlock + ResultMessage; show cost.",
    "(6:49-13:25) Echo agent: run echo_agent.py; it calls a custom echo tool (reverse/uppercase/repeat). @tool decorator + create_sdk_mcp_server (in-memory MCP). Model downgraded to Haiku. ClaudeSDKClient (not query) enables custom tools and, with --follow-up, multi-turn. Then 'list your available tools' reveals the 15 baked-in Claude Code tools STILL loaded — they cost context.",
    "(13:30-21:35) Calc agent: interactive REPL. 'list all tools' shows ONLY the two custom tools because a big disallowed_tools block strips every built-in from context. Convert 55F->C, 125m->mm; compare mortgage deals (multi-tool). resume=session_id gives true conversation memory. Sonnet model; consistent prompts/ directory across all agents.",
    "(21:38-29:45) Social Hype agent: pass keywords + -n notification prompt; it taps the Bluesky firehose WebSocket, deterministically filters ~thousands of messages for keywords, then runs an LLM sentiment analysis (submit_analysis) and fires a macOS alert + TTS (notify) when the criteria match. System prompt carries a static {NOTIFICATION_CRITERIA} variable locked at boot and an embedded workflow.",
    "(29:49-37:05) QA agent: a codebase Q&A expert REPL. 'list your tools' shows 13 tools incl. the Firecrawl MCP server. 'think hard' surfaces a ThinkingBlock; Task subagents run parallel search. A block_env_files hook DENIES reading .env (the agent tries, gets blocked, reports why) — governance in code. Read-only tool set; /qa_agent slash command.",
    "(37:08-42:25) Tri-Copy-Writer: a Vue web UI. One prompt ('write a blog post title 30-60 chars') returns SIX variations at once; drag-drop a file for context. Backend is a FastAPI /copy method wrapping ClaudeSDKClient with {NUMBER_OF_VERSIONS} set by -v; ALL tools disallowed; JSON parsed into a Pydantic response object.",
    "(42:29-50:05) Micro SDLC: a kanban board (Plan->Build->Review->Ship). Create ticket 'update titles', drag to Plan; the Planner agent (preset+append, write-restricted to specs/) plans, hands to the Builder (pure Claude Code preset, bypassPermissions) which edits the app on itself, then the Reviewer (preset+append, write-restricted to reviews/) reviews and it ships — a single out-loop prompt via the Peter framework. Hooks intercept and allow/deny writes live over WebSocket.",
    "(50:11-1:00:00) Ultra Stream (favorite combo): dual-panel UI. A Stream agent continuously reads a JSONL log file in 3-5 line chunks (sed subprocess), summarizes one log per call with severity, raises alerts, and calls clear_context to reset its own window — operating on a file far bigger than its context. An Inspector agent consumes those produced logs to answer 'list issues 1-3', 'find logs for user X', and to notify engineering/support teams.",
    "(1:00:00-1:07:11) Decision framework: build custom agents when you need programmatic agents, repeat workflows, domain-specific wins, cost-down/performance-up, permission checks, or to stay out of the loop — otherwise use out-of-the-box/extended agents (and never try to out-build Claude Code at general engineering). Recap: agentic coding is about what we can teach our agents to do; the path is better, more, then custom agents."
  ],
  "quotes": [
    {
      "text": "Agentic engineering leads every engineer down one single path. Better agents, more agents, and then custom agents.",
      "approx_timestamp": "0:07"
    },
    {
      "text": "The out-of-the-box agents are incredible, but there's a massive problem with these tools. They're built for everyone's codebase, not yours.",
      "approx_timestamp": "0:47"
    },
    {
      "text": "This mismatch can cost you hundreds of hours and millions of tokens, scaling as your codebase grows.",
      "approx_timestamp": "0:56"
    },
    {
      "text": "This is where all the alpha is in engineering. It's in the hard specific problems that most engineers and most agents can't solve out of the box.",
      "approx_timestamp": "1:05"
    },
    {
      "text": "The system prompt is the most important element of your custom agents, with zero exceptions.",
      "approx_timestamp": "5:03"
    },
    {
      "text": "The system prompt affects every single user prompt the agent runs, every single one. So all of your work is multiplied by your system prompt.",
      "approx_timestamp": "5:20"
    },
    {
      "text": "This is not Claude Code anymore. It might be using the same model, but the system prompt is truly what builds the agent.",
      "approx_timestamp": "6:10"
    },
    {
      "text": "Everything that's going into your agent winds up in the context window at some point... It consumes space in your agent's mind.",
      "approx_timestamp": "12:10"
    },
    {
      "text": "It's not the models that are the limitation, the bottleneck anymore. It's you and I. It's our ability to wield better agents, more agents, and now custom agents.",
      "approx_timestamp": "19:42"
    },
    {
      "text": "You can embed workflows in your system prompt if you're sure that that is what you want to happen every single time your agent executes.",
      "approx_timestamp": "27:38"
