+
+      <section class="chapter-section" id="mastery">
+        <span class="section-number">12</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Cumulative mastery · retrieve, trace, diagnose, choose</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">Close the chapter before answering. A recognized phrase earns half credit; a source-grounded explanation that predicts behavior earns full credit.</p>
+
+        <h3>Round 1 · Foundations</h3>
+        <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Foundations retrieval questions">
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. Reconstruct the agentic path.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Better agents → more agents → custom agents. Custom follows prompt/context mastery and scaled compute; it is not the default starting point.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. What are the four fields you locate before reading an agent top to bottom?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Context, model, prompt, and tools—the core four.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. Why can Pong remain Pong across unrelated user requests?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Its complete system-prompt overwrite is injected before every user prompt and constrains the same model to the same narrow behavior.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. State the SDK rename in all three runnable forms.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Package <code>claude-agent-sdk</code>, module <code>claude_agent_sdk</code>, class <code>ClaudeAgentOptions</code>. The recording shows the earlier Claude Code SDK / <code>ClaudeCodeOptions</code> name.</p></details></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <h3>Round 2 · Trace the runtime</h3>
+        <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="SDK runtime retrieval questions">
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">5. When is <code>query()</code> sufficient?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>For a one-off request without the lesson’s custom tools or multi-turn client lifecycle, as in Pong.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">6. What two things does <code>ClaudeSDKClient</code> enable in this lesson?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Custom tools and a continuing conversation/client lifecycle.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">7. What exact value makes Calc’s later call continuous?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The prior <code>ResultMessage.session_id</code>, supplied to the next <code>ClaudeAgentOptions</code> via <code>resume=</code>.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">8. Reconstruct custom tool registration from function to allowed name.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Decorate the async function with <code>@tool</code>; mount it using <code>create_sdk_mcp_server</code>; attach the server in <code>mcp_servers</code>; expose its qualified <code>mcp__server__tool</code> name in <code>allowed_tools</code>.</p></details></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <h3>Round 3 · Diagnose the failure</h3>
+        <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Diagnosis retrieval questions">
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">9. Echo calls only its custom tool but still reports many built-ins. Which assumption failed?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The assumption that <code>allowed_tools</code> removes other definitions from context. It gates calls; explicit <code>disallowed_tools</code> removes listed built-ins.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">10. A custom tool never fires, and the code uses the Pong runtime surface. First repair?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Use <code>ClaudeSDKClient</code>, register the in-memory MCP server, and expose the qualified tool name.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">11. QA can read source files but refuses <code>.env.sample</code>. Is that a prompt or tool absence?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Neither. A <code>PreToolUse</code> hook conditionally denies that specific path class while preserving Read for normal files.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">12. The planner writes application code instead of a specification. What two layers should you inspect?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The appended planner system instructions and the write-path hook that should restrict output to <code>specs/</code>.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">13. Ultra Stream cost grows because every raw log is kept in the prompt. Architectural repair?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Use bounded line-range tools, keep raw data/cache outside the prompt, persist one produced summary per log, and let the Inspector query the produced artifacts.</p></details></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <h3>Round 4 · Transfer and judgment</h3>
+        <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Transfer and judgment retrieval questions">
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">14. Why are planner, builder, and reviewer allowed to use different prompt forms?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Their job shapes differ. Planner/reviewer need role extensions and bounded output; builder’s job already matches the pure engineering preset.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">15. A one-time exploratory refactor needs a human in the loop. Complete custom, extension, or generalist?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Start with the generalist; extend only if a specific role instruction is repeatedly missing. The source decision framework argues against complete custom for in-loop, generic, one-off work.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">16. A repeated log triage workflow needs bounded reads, severity summaries, alerts, and a query UI. Closest template?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Ultra Stream: a producer Stream agent, persisted produced logs, and a consumer Inspector agent with narrow domain tools.</p></details></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <aside class="callout callout--why">
+          <p class="callout__label">Scoring</p>
+          <p><strong>14–16:</strong> ready to teach and template a real specialist. <strong>10–13:</strong> rerun the lab checkpoint attached to each miss. <strong>0–9:</strong> redraw the agentic path and annotate Pong, Echo, and Calc through the core four before returning to the UI agents.</p>
+        </aside>
+
+        <h3>Capstone teach-back</h3>
+        <div class="teach-back">
+          <p>Without notes, teach a colleague for seven minutes:</p>
+          <ol>
+            <li>Why the path is better → more → custom rather than custom first.</li>
+            <li>How overwrite differs from preset+append.</li>
+            <li>What the Echo→Calc contrast proves about tool visibility and context.</li>
+            <li>Why custom tools change the runtime surface and how a session resumes.</li>
+            <li>How QA and Micro SDLC enforce governance with hooks.</li>
+            <li>How Ultra Stream passes structured knowledge from producer to consumer without loading an unbounded source.</li>
+            <li>When you would refuse to build a custom agent.</li>
+          </ol>
+          <p class="fine">Expert pass: your listener can open an unfamiliar lesson agent, locate the options block, predict behavior, and choose whether pure, extended, or custom is appropriate.</p>
+        </div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section" id="glossary">
+        <span class="section-number">13</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">AH-3 glossary</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">Use these terms narrowly. Each definition connects to an observable lesson file, runtime behavior, or configuration choice.</p>
+        <dl class="glossary">
+          <div><dt>Claude Agent SDK</dt><dd>The current name for the programmatic SDK used by all eight agents. The package is <code>claude-agent-sdk</code>; the recording uses the former Claude Code SDK name.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt><code>ClaudeAgentOptions</code></dt><dd>The shipped Python configuration object where the lesson sets context, model, system prompt, tools, hooks, working directory, permission mode, and session resume.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Core four</dt><dd>Context, model, prompt, and tools—the first reading lens for every custom agent.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>System-prompt overwrite</dt><dd>A standalone string that replaces the Claude Code preset and defines a new persistent agent identity, as in Pong.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Preset</dt><dd>The retained <code>claude_code</code> system prompt. The SDLC builder uses it without appended role instructions.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Extended agent</dt><dd>An agent that keeps the preset and adds role instructions through <code>append</code>, often with additional hooks or options.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Complete custom agent</dt><dd>An agent whose system identity is overwritten and whose domain may also add custom tools, constrained context, sessions, and a deployed form factor.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt><code>query()</code></dt><dd>The lesson’s one-off request surface, used by Pong where no custom tools or continuing conversation is needed.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt><code>ClaudeSDKClient</code></dt><dd>The mounted client used for custom tools and continuous interaction.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt><code>resume</code></dt><dd>An option that reconnects a new call with a prior session by using its captured session ID.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt><code>allowed_tools</code></dt><dd>A list that permits specific tool calls. It does not by itself guarantee that every other built-in definition is absent from context.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt><code>disallowed_tools</code></dt><dd>A list that forbids and removes named built-in tool definitions from the agent’s visible context.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Custom tool</dt><dd>A typed async function exposed to the agent with <code>@tool</code>; the model decides when to call it and deterministic code performs the action.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>In-memory MCP server</dt><dd>A server created by <code>create_sdk_mcp_server</code> to mount custom tools directly inside the runtime.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>External MCP server</dt><dd>A configured external tool provider, such as the optional Firecrawl setup used by QA.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Response stream</dt><dd>The sequence of assistant content, thinking, tool calls, tool results, and final run metadata that the wrapper parses for behavior and observability.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Runtime-locked system value</dt><dd>A placeholder substituted when the agent boots and held for that run, such as notification criteria or copy variation count.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Per-call user value</dt><dd>Data supplied with the current query and free to change between turns, such as the current copy request.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Hook</dt><dd>Code executed around tool use to allow, deny, modify, or audit an action.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt><code>PreToolUse</code></dt><dd>A hook stage before execution. QA uses it to deny reads whose paths contain <code>.env</code>.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt><code>PostToolUse</code></dt><dd>A hook stage after execution, suitable for logging and observation of completed calls.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Producer/consumer agents</dt><dd>A multi-agent pattern where one agent creates structured artifacts and another queries or acts on them. Ultra Stream persists produced logs for Inspector.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Self-managed context</dt><dd>The lesson’s pattern of bounded reads plus periodic context/reconnect control so work does not require loading an unbounded source. The exact reset mechanics should be checked in the installed SDK and shipped loop.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Peter framework</dt><dd>The out-of-loop review pattern referenced by the Micro SDLC example: a ticket supplies prompt input and the review system evaluates the implemented work.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Consistent codebase architecture</dt><dd>The repeated agent folder shape—prompts, entrypoint, README, configuration—that reduces navigation cost for humans and agents.</dd></div>
+        </dl>
+      </section>
+
+      <footer class="chapter-footer">
+        <p class="eyebrow">Volume II · Agentic Horizon</p>
+        <div class="chapter-footer__nav">
+          <a href="../index.html">← Book hub</a>
+          <a href="10-seven-levels-agentic-prompt-formats.html">Previous: AH-2 · Seven Levels Agentic Prompt Formats</a>
+        </div>
+      </footer>
+    </main>
+  </div>
+
+  <script>
+    (() => {
+      const topBar = document.getElementById('top-progress');
+      const railBar = document.getElementById('rail-progress');
+      const label = document.getElementById('progress-label');
+      const links = [...document.querySelectorAll('.chapter-nav a')];
+      const sections = links.map(link => document.querySelector(link.getAttribute('href'))).filter(Boolean);
+      let progressFramePending = false;
+
+      const updateProgress = () => {
+        const max = document.documentElement.scrollHeight - window.innerHeight;
+        const value = max > 0 ? Math.min(1, Math.max(0, window.scrollY / max)) : 0;
+        const percent = `${Math.round(value * 100)}%`;
+        topBar.style.width = percent;
+        railBar.style.width = percent;
+        label.textContent = percent;
+      };
+
+      const requestProgressUpdate = () => {
+        if (progressFramePending) return;
+        progressFramePending = true;
+        requestAnimationFrame(() => {
+          updateProgress();
+          progressFramePending = false;
+        });
+      };
+
+      const observer = new IntersectionObserver(entries => {
+        const visible = entries
+          .filter(entry => entry.isIntersecting)
+          .sort((a, b) => b.intersectionRatio - a.intersectionRatio)[0];
+        if (!visible) return;
+        links.forEach(link => {
+          const active = link.getAttribute('href') === `#${visible.target.id}`;
+          if (active) link.setAttribute('aria-current', 'location');
+          else link.removeAttribute('aria-current');
+        });
+      }, { rootMargin: '-20% 0px -65% 0px', threshold: [0, .1, .5] });
+
+      sections.forEach(section => observer.observe(section));
+      updateProgress();
+      window.addEventListener('scroll', requestProgressUpdate, { passive: true });
+      window.addEventListener('resize', requestProgressUpdate);
+    })();
+  </script>
+</body>
+</html>
